<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.5 Directional Derivatives and Gradient
  Vectors.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  The three activities
  keep their task statements and hints; their answers become workspace.
  Content mirrors directional-derivatives.ptx of the full lecture notes;
  every xml:id here is prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: every interactive and animation is kept, since they are how a
  reader checks the answers they have just written by hand, and so are the
  set-up diagrams and the sliced surface.  The one figure that plots the
  four special directions IS the answer to Example III, so it becomes an
  empty grid to draw them on.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-directional-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Directional Derivatives and Gradient Vectors</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand the directional derivative as the rate of change of
<m>f(x,y)</m> in an arbitrary direction, generalizing the partial
derivatives <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>, which are the special cases in the
<m>\hat{\imath}</m> and <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> directions.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret the directional derivative geometrically as the slope of the
tangent line to the curve in which a vertical plane through <m>P_0</m>,
in the direction of <m>\hat{u}</m>, cuts the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand the gradient vector <m>\nabla f</m> and its relationship to
the directional derivative through
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret the directional derivative through the angle between
<m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\hat{u}</m>, and understand why <m>f</m> changes
most rapidly in the direction of the gradient.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand why the gradient vector is perpendicular to the level curves
of <m>f</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
Recall from <xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-partial-derivatives"/> that the partial
derivatives <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> correspond to the rate of change of <m>z = f(x,y)</m> in the <m>\hat{\imath}</m> and
<m>\hat{\jmath}</m> directions. In this section, we generalize this notion
to the rate of change of <m>z = f(x,y)</m> in an arbitrary direction
<m>\hat{u}</m>, which is a unit vector of the form
<m>\hat{u} = u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath}</m>.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-dirderiv-definition">
<title>The Definition of the Directional Derivative</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-directional-derivative">
<title>Directional Derivative</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>derivative of <m>f</m> at <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in the direction
of the unit vector <m>\hat{u} = u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath}</m></term>
is the number
<md xml:id="eq-skel-dirderiv-definition" number="yes">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},\,P_0}
= \lim_{s\to 0}\frac{f(x_0 + su_1,\; y_0 + su_2) - f(x_0,y_0)}{s},
</md>
provided the limit exists. It can alternatively be denoted by
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The geometry behind this definition is best seen in a picture. Starting at
the point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m>, we move a distance <m>s</m> in the
direction of the unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m> to reach a second point, and
we compare the values of the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> above the two
points. Before exploring the figures, write down where that second point
is and what the quotient in <xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>
measures. (The displacement from <m>P_0</m> is <m>s\hat u</m>, so read
its two components off. Then say what the quotient is the slope of, and
what it becomes in the limit. Why must <m>\hat u</m> be a <em>unit</em>
vector for <m>s</m> to be the distance actually travelled?)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.7) -- (15,6.7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.4) -- (15,3.4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {The second point $P =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.4) {The quotient is the slope of \dots, and in the limit \dots};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.1) {Why $\hat u$ must be a unit vector:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the second point of the definition, for what the difference quotient measures, and for why the direction vector must have length one.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The four figures that follow show the construction from complementary
angles: the first lets you explore it in space, the second records the
displacement in the <m>xy</m>-plane, the third animates the secant line
rotating onto the tangent line, and the fourth reports the difference
quotient itself as <m>s</m> shrinks, so that the limit can be watched
rather than only read.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-dirderiv-setup-3d">
<caption>
Interactive view of the definition of the directional derivative. The point <m>P</m> moves the distance <m>s</m> from <m>P_0</m> along <m>\hat{u}</m>, and the secant slope approaches <m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> as <m>s \to 0</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-dirderiv-setup-3d" iframe="dirderiv-setup-3d.html" width="100%" aspect="8:6">
<instructions>
<p>
The translucent blue surface is the graph of <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. In the
<m>xy</m>-plane below it, the point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> is fixed, the
short blue arrow is the unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m>, and the gold arrow
is the displacement <m>s\hat{u}</m>, which ends at the point
<m>P(x_0+su_1,\, y_0+su_2)</m>. The dashed vertical lines rise from
<m>P_0</m> and <m>P</m> to the surface, where the values <m>f(P_0)</m>
and <m>f(P)</m> are marked. Drag the slider to change the distance
<m>s</m>, or press <q>s <m>\to</m> 0</q> to animate the limit in the
definition of the directional derivative: as <m>s \to 0</m>, the point
<m>P</m> slides back toward <m>P_0</m> and the difference quotient
<m>\big(f(P) - f(P_0)\big)/s</m>, displayed above the figure,
approaches <m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>. Drag the figure
to view it from a different angle, or press <q>Rotate</q> to spin it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-dirderiv-setup-prefigure">
<caption>
The two points <m>P_0</m> and <m>P</m> used in the definition of the
directional derivative. As <m>s \to 0</m>, the point <m>P</m> slides
along the direction <m>\hat{u}</m> back toward <m>P_0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
