</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-dirderiv-setup-video">
<caption>
Animation of the definition of the directional derivative. The vertical
plane through <m>P_0</m> in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the
surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> in a curve, and the point
<m>P = P_0 + s\hat{u}</m> moves on this plane. The secant line through
<m>(P_0, f(P_0))</m> and <m>(P, f(P))</m> has slope
<m>\big(f(P) - f(P_0)\big)/s</m>, and as <m>s \to 0</m> it rotates onto
the tangent line, whose slope is the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-dirderiv-setup"
       source="videos/dirderiv_secant_tangent_manim.mp4"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-dirderiv-definition">
<caption>
The difference quotient <m>\big(f(P)-f(P_0)\big)/s</m> for <m>f(x,y)=1+x^2+y^2</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, as functions of the direction angle and the step <m>s</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-dirderiv-definition" iframe="dirderiv-definition.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider for the angle to choose the unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m>,
and drag the slider for <m>s</m> to move the point <m>P</m> along the
direction <m>\hat{u}</m> starting at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, for the function
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>. The readout compares the difference
quotient <m>\big(f(P) - f(P_0)\big)/s</m> with its limit as
<m>s \to 0</m>, which is the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-dirderiv-definition">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Use the definition of directional derivative to compute
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Substitute the given <m>x_0</m>, <m>y_0</m>, <m>u_1</m>, <m>u_2</m>
into <xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-definition"/> and expand. Both squares
are the same, which halves the algebra; after expanding, every surviving
term carries a factor of <m>s</m>, so the quotient simplifies before the
limit is taken. Keep this number <mdash/> you will recover it in two lines
in <xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-gradient"/> once the gradient is
available.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {Substituting and expanding:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the substitution and expansion of the limit and one for the resulting directional derivative.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The computation has a useful geometric picture. Along the ray through
<m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>, the values of <m>f</m>
are a single-variable function <m>g(s)</m>. Write it down, and connect it
to what you just computed. (Substitute the same two expressions into
<m>f</m>, but keep <m>s</m> as a variable rather than letting it go to
zero. Then say which derivative of <m>g</m> your answer above is, and
check it by differentiating <m>g</m> directly.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$g(s) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$g'(0) =$ \qquad which is:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the single-variable function along the ray and one for its derivative at zero and what that derivative is.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-dirderiv-secant-3d-manim">
<caption>
The secant through <m>(P_0, f(P_0))</m> and <m>(P, f(P))</m> on the
surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, rotating onto the tangent line at
<m>P_0</m> as <m>s \to 0</m>, with slope
<m>2\sqrt2 + s \to 2\sqrt2</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-dirderiv-secant-3d-manim" iframe="dirderiv-secant-3d-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Press <q>Play</q> to watch the whole construction in one continuous
motion: the vertical plane through <m>P_0(1,1)</m> in the direction
<m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> in the gold
curve, the red secant joins <m>(P_0, f(P_0))</m> to <m>(P, f(P))</m>,
and as <m>s \to 0</m> that secant rotates onto the green tangent line.
The reading at the lower left compares the secant slope
<m>2\sqrt 2 + s</m> with the tangent slope <m>2\sqrt 2</m>, which is the
directional derivative <m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m>. Stop
the animation at any time and drag the <m>s</m> slider yourself, or
press <q>s <m>\to</m> 0</q> to replay just the limit. Drag the figure to
change the viewpoint, or press <q>Spin</q> to let it turn.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

<activity xml:id="activity-skel-dirderiv-partials">
<title>The Partial Derivatives Are Directional Derivatives</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<xref ref="def-skel-directional-derivative"/> measures the rate of change
of <m>f</m> in an arbitrary direction <m>\hat{u}</m>. Two directions are
already familiar from <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>. In this activity you will
check that the definition reproduces exactly those two derivatives when
<m>\hat{u}</m> points along a coordinate axis. Use only
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>; nothing beyond the definition
is needed.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-skel-dirderiv-i">
<statement>
<p>
Take <m>\hat{u} = \hat{\imath}</m>, so that <m>u_1 = 1</m> and
<m>u_2 = 0</m>. Substitute these into
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-definition"/> and simplify. Which
single-variable limit is left, and what is its value?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,6.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for substituting the first standard unit vector into the definition.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Ask yourself which of the two inputs of <m>f</m> actually changes as
<m>s</m> varies.
</p>
</hint>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-dirderiv-j">
<statement>
<p>
Repeat the computation with <m>\hat{u} = \hat{\jmath}</m>, and state the
conclusion.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,6.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the same computation with the second standard unit vector.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-dirderiv-check">
<statement>
<p>
Test this on the function of <xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>.
With <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> and <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, compute
<m>\left(D_{\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> straight from the
definition, then compute <m>f_x(1,1)</m> in the usual way and compare.
Compare both with the value found in
<xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>: which direction climbs
fastest, and does that match your intuition about the surface?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {From the definition, and by differentiating:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Comparison with the diagonal direction:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the two computations of the same derivative and one for the comparison with the diagonal direction.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-dirderiv-negative">
<statement>
<p>
Finally, take <m>\hat{u} = -\hat{\imath}</m>. Show that
<m>\left(D_{-\hat{\imath}} f\right)_{P_0} = -f_x(x_0,y_0)</m>, and say in
a sentence why this is what you should expect.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {The computation (try the substitution $h = -s$):};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Why this is expected:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the computation with the reversed direction and one for the reason the sign flips.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<conclusion>
<p>
Every partial derivative is a directional derivative, so nothing already
known about <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> is lost. What the definition adds is
the freedom to point <m>\hat{u}</m> anywhere.
</p>
</conclusion>

</activity>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-gradient-vector">
<title>The Gradient Vector</title>

<p>
Evaluating that limit every time would be painful, and the chain rule
removes the need to. Note first that the line through <m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m>
in the direction <m>\hat{u}</m> can be written as
<md xml:id="eq-skel-line-through-P0" number="yes">
x(s) = x_0 + su_1, \qquad y(s) = y_0 + su_2.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Use that, together with the chain rule, to find a formula for the
directional derivative. (This is
<xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-two"/> with <m>s</m> in place of <m>t</m>,
and the dependency diagram is
<xref ref="fig-skel-chain-rule-tree-prefigure"/>. Differentiate
<xref ref="eq-skel-line-through-P0"/> to get the two easy factors, and
then <em>recognize the result as a dot product</em> <mdash/> that last step
is the whole point, so write the two vectors out separately.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$dx/ds =$ \qquad\qquad $dy/ds =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {By the chain rule, $\dfrac{df}{ds} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {As a dot product:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the two derivatives along the line, for the chain rule expression, and for the same expression written as a dot product.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-gradient-vector">
<title>Gradient Vector</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>gradient vector</term> of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> at
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-skel-gradient-definition" number="yes">
\left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)_{P_0}\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)_{P_0}\hat{\jmath}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
The gradient vector and the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> at
<m>P_0(x_0,y_0)</m> in the direction
<m>\hat{u} = u_1\hat{\imath} + u_2\hat{\jmath}</m> are related via the
following dot product:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-dirderiv-gradient-relation" number="yes">
\left(\frac{df}{ds}\right)_{\hat{u},\,P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-chain-rule-tree-prefigure">
<caption>
The chain rule dependency diagram: multiplying down each branch and
adding the two products gives
<m>\frac{df}{ds} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{ds}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{ds}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="48%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-chain-rule-tree">
<diagram dimensions="(300,280)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-3.0,-0.7,3.0,3.7)">
<label at="ct-z" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>z=f(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-x" anchor="(-1.7,1.5)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-y" anchor="(1.7,1.5)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-s" anchor="(0,-0.1)" alignment="c" scale="1.4">
<m>s</m>
</label>
<vector at="ct-zx" v="(-1.1,-1.05)" tail="(-0.45,2.85)" stroke="#0072B2"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="ct-zy" v="(1.1,-1.05)" tail="(0.45,2.85)" stroke="#0072B2"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="ct-xs" v="(1.25,-1.05)" tail="(-1.5,1.25)" stroke="#D55E00"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="ct-ys" v="(-1.25,-1.05)" tail="(1.5,1.25)" stroke="#D55E00"
        thickness="2"/>
<label at="ct-dfdx" anchor="(-1.75,2.55)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-dfdy" anchor="(1.75,2.55)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-dxds" anchor="(-1.6,0.55)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{dx}{ds}</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-dyds" anchor="(1.6,0.55)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00" scale="1.4">
<m>\dfrac{dy}{ds}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A dependency tree for the chain rule. At the top is z equals f of x comma y. Blue arrows labeled partial f partial x and partial f partial y lead down to the intermediate variables x and y. Red arrows labeled d x d s and d y d s lead from x and y down to the single independent variable s.">
  <annotation ref="ct-z" text="The dependent variable z equals f of x comma y at the top of the tree."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-zx" text="The branch from z to x, labeled with the partial derivative of f with respect to x."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-zy" text="The branch from z to y, labeled with the partial derivative of f with respect to y."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-xs" text="The branch from x down to s, labeled d x d s, which equals u 1 along the line through P 0."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-ys" text="The branch from y down to s, labeled d y d s, which equals u 2 along the line through P 0."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-s" text="The independent variable s, the arc length along the line through P 0 in the direction u."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-gradient-perp-level-curve">
<title>The Gradient is Perpendicular to the Level Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y)</m> be a differentiable function, and let <m>C</m> be the
level curve <m>f(x,y) = c</m> for a constant <m>c</m>. At every point of
<m>C</m> where <m>\nabla f \neq \mathbf 0</m>, the gradient vector
<m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curve <m>C</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Prove it. (Parametrize <m>C</m> as
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \langle x(t), y(t)\rangle</m>. The one fact you have is
that <m>f</m> is <em>constant</em> along <m>C</m>; differentiate that
statement with respect to <m>t</m> and see what the right-hand side
becomes. Then recognize the left-hand side as a dot product of two
vectors you can name, and say what a vanishing dot product means. Finish
by explaining why the velocity vector is tangent to <m>C</m>, and where
the hypothesis <m>\nabla f \neq \mathbf 0</m> is needed.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12.5) -- (15,12.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,15.7) {Along $C$, $f(x(t),y(t)) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.2) {Differentiating:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {As a dot product:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {Conclusion, and where $\nabla f \neq \mathbf 0$ is used:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the constancy of the function along the level curve, for differentiating it, for recognizing the dot product, and for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-gradient-perp-prefigure">
<caption>
The gradient <m>\nabla f</m> meets the level curve <m>C\colon f = c</m>
at a right angle: it is perpendicular to the velocity vector
<m>\mathbf v(t)</m>, which is tangent to <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-gradient-perp">
<diagram dimensions="(360,320)" margins="10">
<definition>ce(t) = (2.4*cos(t), 1.6*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>ci(t) = (1.63*cos(t), 1.09*sin(t))</definition>
<definition>co(t) = (3.12*cos(t), 2.08*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.7,-2.7,3.7,2.9)">
<parametric-curve at="gp-inner" function="ci" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F"/>
<parametric-curve at="gp-outer" function="co" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F"/>
<parametric-curve at="gp-C" function="ce" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="gp-ra1" endpoints="((1.470,1.510),(1.271,1.603))"
      stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="gp-ra2" endpoints="((1.271,1.603),(1.178,1.404))"
      stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
<vector at="gp-grad" v="(0.465,0.997)" tail="(1.377,1.311)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="gp-vel" v="(-0.997,0.465)" tail="(1.377,1.311)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="gp-P" p="(1.377,1.311)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>P</m>
</point>
<label at="gp-grad-label" anchor="(1.95,2.4)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-vel-label" anchor="(0.3,1.85)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf v(t)</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-C-label" anchor="(0,-1.6)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes" color="#009E73">
<m>C\colon f=c</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-outer-label" anchor="(0,-2.08)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes">
<m>f=c+\Delta c</m>
</label>
<label at="gp-inner-label" anchor="(0,-1.09)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes">
<m>f=c-\Delta c</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three nested oval level curves of f. The middle green oval is the level curve C where f equals c. At a point P on C, a blue arrow is the velocity vector v of t, tangent to C, and a red arrow is the gradient of f, perpendicular to the velocity and pointing outward toward the larger level curve. A small square at P marks the right angle between them.">
  <annotation ref="gp-C" text="The level curve C, where f equals c, drawn in green."/>
  <annotation ref="gp-P" text="A point P on the level curve C."/>
  <annotation ref="gp-vel" text="The velocity vector v of t, drawn in blue, tangent to the level curve and pointing in the direction of motion along it."/>
  <annotation ref="gp-grad" text="The gradient vector of f, drawn in red, perpendicular to the velocity vector and pointing outward toward the level curve of larger value."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<activity xml:id="activity-skel-gradient-perp-check">
<title>Checking that the Gradient Meets Level Curves at Right Angles</title>

<introduction>
<p>
<xref ref="thm-skel-gradient-perp-level-curve"/> claims that
<m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curve through a point. This
activity asks you to verify that claim by hand at one point, and then to
test it at many points using the interactive figure that follows.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-skel-perp-level-curve">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>. Find the level curve of <m>f</m>
through <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and give a vector tangent to that curve at
<m>P_0</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {The level curve:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {A tangent vector at $P_0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the level curve through the point and one for a tangent vector to it.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
Level curves of this <m>f</m> are circles centred at the origin. A
tangent to a circle is perpendicular to its radius.
</p>
</hint>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-perp-dot-product">
<statement>
<p>
Compute <m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}</m> and then the dot product of
that gradient with your tangent vector. What does the result say?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} =$ \qquad dot product $=$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Which means:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the gradient and the dot product and one for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-perp-general-point">
<statement>
<p>
Repeat the argument at an arbitrary point <m>(a,b) \ne (0,0)</m> to show
the right angle is not a coincidence of the point <m>(1,1)</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for repeating the orthogonality check at a general point.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-perp-interactive">
<statement>
<p>
Now drag the red point in
<xref ref="fig-skel-int-gradient-level-curves"/> to several locations,
including points on the axes and points far from the origin. Confirm that
the gradient arrow always meets the level curve at a right angle, and
describe how the length of the arrow changes as the point moves away from
the origin. Can you place the point where the gradient vanishes?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-gradient-level-curves">
<caption>
The gradient <m>\nabla f</m> of <m>f(x,y)=1+x^2+y^2</m> at a movable point, shown with the level curve through that point, illustrating that <m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curves.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-gradient-level-curves" iframe="gradient-level-curves.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the red point anywhere in the plane. The gradient vector
<m>\nabla f = 2x\,\hat{\imath} + 2y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> of
<m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> is drawn at the point, together with the
level curve of <m>f</m> through the point. Observe that the gradient is
always perpendicular to the level curve and points in the direction in
which <m>f</m> increases.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {How the arrow length changes, and why:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Where the gradient vanishes, and what happens there:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for how the gradient's length varies with position and one for the point at which it vanishes.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

</activity>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-dirderiv-gradient">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, the point
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>, and the unit vector
<m>\hat{u} = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\imath} + \dfrac{1}{\sqrt 2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Evaluate the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> and interpret your result.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Same function, point and direction as
<xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>, but now use
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/>: find <m>\nabla f</m>,
evaluate it at <m>P_0</m>, and dot it with <m>\hat u</m>. It should take
two lines and agree with the limit you computed earlier. Then read the
answer geometrically off
<xref ref="fig-skel-dirderiv-surface-tikz"/>: <m>\hat u</m> points along a
line in the <m>xy</m>-plane, that line lies in a vertical plane, and that
plane cuts the surface in a curve <mdash/> name the curve, and say which
line's slope your number is.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$\nabla f =$ \qquad\qquad $\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {$\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Geometric interpretation:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the gradient and its value at the point, for the directional derivative, and for the geometric interpretation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-dirderiv-surface-tikz">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> cut by the vertical plane
<m>x - y = 0</m>, which contains the direction <m>\hat{u}</m>. The
intersection is a parabola, and the directional derivative
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{(1,1)} = 2\sqrt 2</m> is the slope of
its tangent line at the point <m>(1,1,3)</m>. The level curve
<m>f = 3</m> through <m>(1,1)</m>, the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m>, is
drawn on the surface at height <m>z = 3</m>, with its dashed projection
in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-skel-dirderiv-surface-tikz" width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A paraboloid cut by a vertical plane, with the trace parabola and its
tangent line at the point one comma one comma three.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The surface <m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, an upward-opening paraboloid,
drawn as a translucent blue surface. A translucent yellow vertical
plane <m>x - y = 0</m> passes through the surface above the line
<m>y = x</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, where the unit vector
<m>\hat{u}</m> is drawn as a blue arrow starting at <m>(1,1,0)</m>.
The plane meets the surface in a parabola, drawn in solid vermillion,
and a green tangent line touches this parabola at the marked point
<m>(1,1,3)</m>. The slope of this tangent line, measured within the
vertical plane, is the directional derivative <m>2\sqrt 2</m>. A
reddish-purple circle on the surface at height <m>z = 3</m> marks the
level curve <m>f = 3</m>, and its dashed projection in the
<m>xy</m>-plane is the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2</m> through the point
<m>(1,1)</m>.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oiyellow}{RGB}{240,228,66}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=11.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-2.1, ymax=2.1, zmin=0, zmax=4.9,
      clip=false,
      legend style={
        at={(0.5,1.04)}, anchor=south,
        legend columns=2,
        font=\scriptsize,
        draw=black!35, rounded corners=2pt,
        fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
        inner sep=4pt, row sep=2pt, column sep=10pt,
      },
      legend cell align=left,
      legend image post style={line width=1.4pt, sharp plot},
    ]
    % ---- vertical plane x - y = 0 (drawn first, behind surface) ----
    \fill[oiyellow, opacity=0.28]
      (axis cs:-1.7,-1.7,0) -- (axis cs:1.7,1.7,0)
      -- (axis cs:1.7,1.7,4.4) -- (axis cs:-1.7,-1.7,4.4) -- cycle;
    % ---- surface: paraboloid z = 1 + x^2 + y^2 ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!12!white) color=(oiblue!38!white)},
              opacity=0.5, fill opacity=0.5,
              domain=0:360, samples=61,
              y domain=0:1.7, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {1 + y^2});
    % surface listed in the legend (so the equation never overlaps the plot)
    \addlegendimage{area legend, fill=oiblue!30, draw=oiblue!45}
    \addlegendentry{surface $z=1+x^2+y^2$}
    % light parallels (circles) for shape cues
    \foreach \rr in {0.5,0.9,1.3,1.7}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:360,
          samples=97, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({\rr*cos(x)}, {\rr*sin(x)}, {1 + \rr*\rr});}\tmp}
    % ---- trace: parabola z = 1 + 2t^2 along x = y = t ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.7pt, oivermillion, domain=-1.2:1.2,
        samples=61, samples y=0]
      (x, x, {1 + 2*x^2});
    \addlegendentry{trace in the plane $x-y=0$}
    % ---- tangent line at (1,1,3) ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.7pt, oigreen, domain=-0.55:0.55,
        samples=2, samples y=0]
      ({1 + x/sqrt(2)}, {1 + x/sqrt(2)}, {3 + 2*sqrt(2)*x});
    \addlegendentry{tangent line, slope $2\sqrt2$}
    % ---- level curve f = 3 through (1,1): x^2 + y^2 = 2 ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.6pt, oipurple, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0]
      ({sqrt(2)*cos(x)}, {sqrt(2)*sin(x)}, 3);
    \addlegendentry{level curve $x^2+y^2=2$}
    \addplot3[line width=1.2pt, oipurple, dashed, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({sqrt(2)*cos(x)}, {sqrt(2)*sin(x)}, 0);
    % ---- dashed drop line and marked point ----
    \addplot3[black!60, dashed, line width=0.6pt, samples y=0, forget plot]
      coordinates {(1,1,0) (1,1,3)};
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=black, forget plot]
      coordinates {(1,1,3)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.1,1.1,3.3) {$(1,1,3)$};
    % ---- unit vector u in the xy-plane ----
    \draw[-{Latex}, oiblue, line width=1.5pt]
      (axis cs:1,1,0) -- (axis cs:1.5,1.5,0);
    \node[oiblue, anchor=north west, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.45,1.45,0)
      {$\hat{u}=\left\langle\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2},\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:1,1,-0.12) {$(1,1)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-dirderiv-surface-slice-manim">
<caption>
The same slice, animated: the tangent to the sliced surface at
<m>(1,1,3)</m> tipping from steepest ascent through level to steepest
descent as the direction <m>\hat{u}</m> turns around <m>P_0(1,1)</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-dirderiv-surface-slice-manim" iframe="dirderiv-surface-slice-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Press <q>Play</q> to watch the vertical plane through <m>P_0(1,1)</m>
turn once around. The gold curve is where the plane cuts the surface
<m>z = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m>, and the straight line through <m>(1,1,3)</m>
is the tangent to that curve; its slope is
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\cdot\hat{u}</m>,
read off at the lower left. The tangent is green and steepest upwards
when <m>\hat{u}</m> points along <m>\nabla f</m>, red and steepest
downwards along <m>-\nabla f</m>, and yellow and level in the two
directions tangent to the pink level curve. Stop the animation at any
time and drag the <m>\varphi</m> slider, drag the figure to change the
viewpoint, or press <q>Spin</q>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-dirderiv-properties">
<title>Directions of Fastest Increase, Fastest Decrease, and No Change</title>

<p>
Rewrite the directional derivative so that the direction enters through a
single angle. (Start from
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/> and expand the dot
product geometrically, as in <xref ref="eq-skel-angle"/>, with
<m>\theta</m> the angle between <m>\nabla f</m> and <m>\hat u</m>. One
factor then drops out; say which and why.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$D_{\hat u} f =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for rewriting the directional derivative in terms of the angle between the gradient and the direction.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-dirderiv-cos-theta">
<title>The Directional Derivative and the Angle</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-dirderiv-cos-theta" number="yes">
D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Only the factor <m>\cos\theta</m> depends on the direction, and it runs
over <m>[-1,1]</m> as <m>\hat{u}</m> turns through the circle of
directions at <m>P_0</m>. Its three extreme values single out three
directions, drawn in
<xref ref="fig-skel-dirderiv-angle-prefigure"/>. Fill them in.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$\cos\theta = 1$: \quad direction: \qquad\qquad $D_{\hat u} f =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$\cos\theta = -1$: \quad direction: \qquad\qquad $D_{\hat u} f =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {$\cos\theta = 0$: \quad direction: \qquad\qquad $D_{\hat u} f =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, one for each extreme value of the cosine, giving the corresponding direction and the value of the directional derivative.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-dirderiv-angle-prefigure">
<caption>
Directions around <m>P_0</m>: <m>f</m> increases fastest along
<m>\nabla f</m>, decreases fastest along <m>-\nabla f</m>, and does not
change in the two directions orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-dirderiv-angle">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-1.9,1.9,1.9)">
<circle at="an-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        dash="6 4" fill="none"/>
<vector at="an-grad" v="(1.405,0.655)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#D55E00"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="an-u" v="(0.087,0.996)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2"
        thickness="3"/>
<vector at="an-zero1" v="(-0.423,0.906)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        thickness="2"/>
<vector at="an-zero2" v="(0.423,-0.906)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#7F7F7F"
        thickness="2"/>
<point at="an-P0" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>P_0</m>
</point>
<label at="an-grad-label" anchor="(1.5,0.8)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\nabla f</m>
</label>
<label at="an-u-label" anchor="(0.09,1.1)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>\hat{u}</m>
</label>
<label at="an-zero1-label" anchor="(-0.55,1.15)" alignment="nw">
<m>D_{\hat{u}}f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="an-zero2-label" anchor="(0.55,-1.15)" alignment="se">
<m>D_{\hat{u}}f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="an-formula" anchor="(0,-1.62)" alignment="c">
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A point P 0 surrounded by a dashed unit circle of possible directions. A long red arrow shows the gradient of f. A blue unit arrow shows a direction u making an angle theta with the gradient. Two gray arrows orthogonal to the gradient mark the directions of zero change. Below the circle is the formula: the directional derivative equals the magnitude of the gradient times cosine theta.">
  <annotation ref="an-grad" text="The gradient vector of f at P 0, drawn in red. Moving in this direction gives the most rapid increase, with directional derivative equal to the magnitude of the gradient."/>
  <annotation ref="an-u" text="A unit vector u, drawn in blue, making an angle theta with the gradient. Its directional derivative is the magnitude of the gradient times cosine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="an-zero1" text="A direction orthogonal to the gradient, where cosine theta is zero, so the directional derivative is zero and f does not change."/>
  <annotation ref="an-zero2" text="The opposite orthogonal direction, where the directional derivative is also zero."/>
  <annotation ref="an-circle" text="The dashed unit circle of all possible directions u at the point P 0."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<activity xml:id="activity-skel-verify-cos-theta">
<title>Verifying the Three Special Directions</title>

<introduction>
<p>
The three cases above are all consequences of the single formula
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-cos-theta"/>. In this activity you will derive
them yourself and then check your answers against
<xref ref="fig-skel-int-gradient-direction-angle"/>, where <m>\hat{u}</m>
can be dragged around the circle of directions.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-skel-cos-theta-formula">
<statement>
<p>
Beginning with <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \nabla f \cdot \hat{u}</m>, explain why
the dot product can be rewritten as <m>\|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>. What
would go wrong if <m>\hat u</m> were not a unit vector?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {The rewriting:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {If $\hat u$ were not a unit vector:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the rewriting of the dot product and one for what fails without a unit vector.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-cos-theta-extremes">
<statement>
<p>
Treat <m>\|\nabla f\|</m> as fixed and let <m>\theta</m> run over
<m>[0, 2\pi)</m>. What are the largest and smallest values of
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m>, and at which angles do they occur? For which angles
is <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = 0</m>?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {Largest and smallest, and where:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Zero when $\theta =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the extreme values with their angles and one for the angles at which the directional derivative vanishes.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-cos-theta-concrete">
<statement>
<p>
Now take <m>f(x,y) = 1 + x^2 + y^2</m> at <m>P_0(1,1)</m>, the function of
<xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>. Compute <m>\nabla f</m> and
<m>\|\nabla f\|</m> at <m>P_0</m>, write <m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> as a
function of <m>\theta</m>, and give the three special unit vectors
explicitly.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} =$ \qquad $\|\nabla f\| =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {$D_{\hat u} f(\theta) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {The three special unit vectors:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the gradient and its length, for the directional derivative as a function of the angle, and for the three special unit vectors.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-skel-cos-theta-check">
<statement>
<p>
Check your work against the figure below. Drag <m>\hat{u}</m> around the
circle and confirm that the meter agrees with your formula at several
angles. Then use the three buttons and compare the readings with your
answers to the previous task. As you drag, at what angle does the reading
change most quickly, and at what angle is it momentarily stationary?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-gradient-direction-angle">
<caption>
The directional derivative <m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m> as the unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m> rotates around <m>P_0</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-gradient-direction-angle" iframe="gradient-direction-angle.html" width="100%" aspect="7:6">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the blue unit vector <m>\hat{u}</m> around the circle of
directions and watch the meter display
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>. Use the buttons to snap
<m>\hat{u}</m> to the three special cases: the same direction as
<m>\nabla f</m> (fastest increase), the opposite direction (fastest
decrease), and a direction orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m> (no change).
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {Changes fastest at $\theta =$ \qquad because:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Stationary at $\theta =$ \qquad which means, for a walker:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the angle at which the reading changes fastest and one for the angle at which it is stationary, with an interpretation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</task>

</activity>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-gradient-directions">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{x^2}{2} + \dfrac{y^2}{2}</m>. Find the directions in
which, at the point <m>P_0(1,1)</m>,
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> increases most rapidly,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> decreases most rapidly,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
there is no change in <m>f</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compute <m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}</m> first; parts
<alert>A</alert> and <alert>B</alert> then follow from the list above, but
the question asks for <em>directions</em>, so normalize. For part
<alert>C</alert> set <m>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)}\cdot\hat u = 0</m>
and solve together with <m>\|\hat u\| = 1</m>; there are two answers, and
they are related in a way worth stating. Finally draw all four directions
on the grid, together with the level curve through <m>P_0</m>, and say how
each is placed relative to that curve.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="52% 44%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (8,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11) -- (8,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (8,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (8,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,13.7) {$\left(\nabla f\right)_{(1,1)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,10.7) {A.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,8.2) {B.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,2.7) {C.};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the gradient at the point and for each of the three parts of the question.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-0.4,-0.4) grid[step=0.25] (2.2,2.2);
\draw[->, thick] (-0.4,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.4) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {1,2} \draw (\x,-0.05) -- (\x,0.05) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2} \draw (-0.05,\y) -- (0.05,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\fill (1,1) circle (1.2pt) node[below right=1pt, font=\scriptsize] {$P_0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Coordinate grid from negative one half to two and a quarter in both directions with the point P nought marked at one comma one, for drawing the level curve and the four special directions.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-gradient-max-directions-manim">
<caption>
The same three directions, animated: <m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m> sweeping
between <m>\|\nabla f\|</m> and <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m> as <m>\hat{u}</m>
turns around <m>P_0(1,1)</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-gradient-max-directions-manim" iframe="gradient-max-directions-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Press <q>Play</q> to watch <m>\hat{u}</m> turn once around
<m>P_0(1,1)</m>. The reading at the lower left follows
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \nabla f\cdot\hat{u} = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m>
as it sweeps from <m>\|\nabla f\|</m> down to <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m> and
back: it is largest along <m>\nabla f</m> (A), smallest along
<m>-\nabla f</m> (B), and zero in the two directions tangent to the
highlighted level curve <m>f = 1</m> (C), where the second vector
<m>\tilde{\hat{u}} = -\hat{u}</m> appears. Stop the animation at any
time and drag <m>\hat{u}</m> on the picture, or use the slider.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-gradient-max-directions-3d-manim">
<caption>
The same 3D view, animated: the tangent to the sliced surface at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> tipping from steepest ascent through level to steepest
descent as <m>\hat{u}</m> turns.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-gradient-max-directions-3d-manim" iframe="gradient-max-directions-3d-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Press <q>Play</q> to watch the vertical plane through <m>P_0(1,1)</m>
turn once around. The gold curve is where it cuts the surface
<m>z = \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{y^2}{2}</m>, and the straight line through
<m>(1,1,1)</m> is the tangent to that curve, whose slope is
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f</m>: it is green and steepest upwards along
<m>\nabla f</m> (A), red and steepest downwards along <m>-\nabla f</m>
(B), and yellow and level in the two directions tangent to the pink
level curve <m>f = 1</m> (C). Stop the animation at any time and drag
the <m>\varphi</m> slider, drag the figure to change the viewpoint, or
press <q>Spin</q>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-gradient-summary">
<title>Summary</title>

<p>
We collect here the main ideas of this section.
</p>

<figure xml:id="video-skel-dirderiv-2d-3d">
<caption>
A directional derivative viewed together in the plane and in space: the unit
vector <m>\hat{u}</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane selects a slice of
the surface <m>z=f(x,y)</m>, and <m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> is the
slope of the resulting tangent line to that slice.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-dirderiv-definition-2d-3d"
       source="videos/dirderiv_definition_2D_3D.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<em>The directional derivative.</em>
<xref ref="def-skel-directional-derivative"/> defines
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}</m> as the limit in
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>; it measures the rate of change
of <m>f</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the direction of a unit vector
<m>\hat{u}</m>. It is computed directly from this limit in
<xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-definition"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The gradient vector.</em> Using the chain rule we obtain
<xref ref="def-skel-gradient-vector"/>, which gives the shortcut
<m>\left(D_{\hat{u}} f\right)_{P_0}
= \left(\nabla f\right)_{P_0}\cdot\hat{u}</m> in
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-gradient-relation"/>. This is applied in
<xref ref="ex-skel-dirderiv-gradient"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>Fastest increase, fastest decrease, and no change.</em> Writing
<m>D_{\hat{u}} f = \|\nabla f\|\cos\theta</m> in
<xref ref="eq-skel-dirderiv-cos-theta"/> shows that <m>f</m> increases
most rapidly in the direction of <m>\nabla f</m>, decreases most rapidly
in the direction of <m>-\nabla f</m>, and does not change in the
directions orthogonal to <m>\nabla f</m>. These directions are found in
<xref ref="ex-skel-gradient-directions"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The gradient is perpendicular to the level curves.</em>
<xref ref="thm-skel-gradient-perp-level-curve"/> proves that
<m>\nabla f</m> is perpendicular to the level curve through each point.
Equivalently, the directions of no change are tangent to the level curve,
while the directions of most rapid increase and decrease are
perpendicular to it.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

</subsection>

<xi:include href="subsec-skel-fmv-scalar-fields.ptx"/>

</section>
